
1. Kubernetes Networking Model

One of Kubernetes' biggest strengths is its networking model.

Basic principle:

Every Pod gets its own IP.

Pods communicate directly without NAT.

Pods can communicate across nodes.

Applications do not need to know where another Pod is running.


Networking should remain transparent to developers.


---

2. Cluster Networking

Communication occurs at multiple levels:

External Client
        │
        ▼
      Route
        │
        ▼
     Service
        │
        ▼
      Pod A
        │
        ▼
      Pod B

Networking happens through Kubernetes resources rather than hardcoded IP addresses.


---

3. Pod Networking

Every Pod receives:

Unique IP address

Shared network namespace (within same pod)

localhost communication between containers

Dynamic IP allocation


Important:

Never hardcode Pod IPs.

Pods may restart and receive different IPs.


---

4. Node Networking

A cluster consists of multiple nodes.

Example from training:

Master01   → 10.128.0.0/24
Master02   → 10.129.0.0/24
Master03   → 10.130.0.0/24

Worker01   → 10.131.0.0/24
Worker02   → 10.128.2.0/24
Worker03   → 10.129.2.0/24

Kubernetes automatically manages node-to-node communication.

Developers do not configure routing manually.


---

5. Pod-to-Pod Communication

Pods can communicate:

On same node

Across different nodes


Example

Worker 1

Pod A
    │
    │
    ▼

Worker 2

Pod B

Communication works automatically through Kubernetes networking.


---

6. Container Network Interface (CNI)

Kubernetes itself does not implement networking.

Networking is provided through CNI plugins.

Examples:

Flannel

Calico

Weave

OVN-Kubernetes (default in modern OpenShift)


Trainer mentioned:

Node-to-node communication is handled by Kubernetes/OS,

but Pod networking is implemented using CNI plugins.


---

7. Flannel

Flannel creates an overlay network between nodes.

Characteristics:

Uses VXLAN tunneling

Easy to configure

Supports Pod-to-Pod communication

Supports Pod-to-Service communication


Limitations:

No Network Policies

Uses UDP encapsulation

Slightly slower due to packet encapsulation


Trainer note:

Flannel is easier to understand.


---

8. Calico

Calico is another CNI plugin.

Advantages:

High performance

Uses BGP

Supports Network Policies

Easier packet debugging

Better enterprise choice


Trainer mentioned:

Can integrate well with packet analysis tools.


---

9. Weave

Weave creates a mesh network.

Characteristics:

Uses UDP

Simple architecture

Easy to understand


Limitation:

More difficult to troubleshoot compared to Calico.


---

10. Kubernetes Services

Pods are temporary.

Services provide stable networking.

Types covered:


---

ClusterIP

Default service.

Accessible only within cluster.

Typical use:

Frontend
      │
      ▼

Backend API

      │
      ▼

Database

Database should generally be exposed only using ClusterIP.


---

NodePort

Exposes service on every worker node.

Example:

WorkerIP:32000

Even if Pod moves,

NodePort remains available.

Useful for:

Testing

Labs

Small environments


Trainer command:

oc describe svc <service>

shows allocated NodePort.


---

LoadBalancer

Used mostly in cloud.

AWS

Azure

GCP

Cloud provider assigns external IP.

Example:

Internet

↓

LoadBalancer

↓

Service

↓

Pods


---

11. MetalLB

Bare-metal clusters cannot automatically assign external IPs.

Trainer demonstrated MetalLB.

MetalLB:

Assigns external IPs

Works with LoadBalancer services

Advertises IPs using BGP


Without MetalLB:

EXTERNAL-IP

<pending>

After MetalLB:

EXTERNAL-IP

192.168.x.x


---

12. Ingress

Ingress is a Kubernetes resource.

Purpose:

HTTP/HTTPS routing.

Needs:

Ingress Resource

Ingress Controller

Load Balancer


Example controllers:

NGINX

HAProxy

F5



---

13. OpenShift Routes

OpenShift simplifies external access using Routes.

Browser

↓

Route

↓

Service

↓

Pods

Routes are built on Ingress concepts but provide a simpler developer experience.


---

14. TLS Termination

Trainer discussed Edge Routing.

Flow:

Browser

HTTPS

↓

Bank Reverse Proxy

↓

HTTP

↓

Application

TLS terminates at the reverse proxy.

Internal communication can remain HTTP.

Benefits:

Easier certificate management

Reduced complexity

Better performance inside cluster



---

15. Reverse Proxy

Purpose:

SSL termination

Load balancing

Request forwarding

Security


Banks commonly terminate TLS before traffic enters OpenShift.


---

16. DNS

Applications should access services using:

service-name

or

service-name.namespace.svc.cluster.local

Never use Pod IPs.


---

17. kube-proxy

Runs on every worker node.

Responsibilities:

Service networking

Load balancing

Forwarding traffic to Pods


Developers rarely interact with it directly.


---

18. Scheduling & Networking

When Pods restart:

Pod IP changes

Node may change

Service remains same


Applications should always communicate through Services.

Never through Pod IP.


---

19. Network Policies

Supported by:

Calico

OVN Kubernetes


Not supported by Flannel.

Purpose:

Control which Pods can communicate.

Example:

Frontend

↓

Backend

↓

Database

Reject all other traffic.


---

20. Best Practices

✔ Never hardcode Pod IPs

✔ Always communicate through Services

✔ Use Routes for external access

✔ Keep databases behind ClusterIP

✔ Prefer DNS names

✔ Use Network Policies

✔ Let scheduler choose nodes

✔ Don't assume Pod location


